ss density at each point is proportional to its distance from the $y$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $y=2.$" 13194139535857 true] [17592186370685 1113 #uuid "1b65c72c-7c28-579f-b489-369412372954" 13194139535857 true] [17592186370685 1145 "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070210/assets/f5a73161415455032a7fd2031dba8f1c56a7ccb40bf6dc3e4fa405a69aee74d8.png)\n\nAlso, the distance between a point and the line $y=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|y-2|.\n$$\n\nNext, the mass density can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k|x|=kx,$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find the value of $k$ by integrating the mass density function over the plate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}kx\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}x[y]_{{x}^{2}}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4x-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{[2{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =k(8-4) \\\\  & =4k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=kx=\\frac{M}{4}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $y=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}\\frac{M}{4}x\\cdot |y-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}\\frac{M}{4}x\\cdot (y-2